---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="pt-20 pb-32 lg:pt-30 lg:pb-40">
    <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl xl:text-7xl font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-tighter max-w-[820px] mb-12">
      The platform moat is gone.<br/>
      <span class="text-fg-tertiary">Distribution has to be portable.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary max-w-[560px] mb-8">
      Bird Candy is a product strategy studio for the open protocol shift. We
      help product leaders decide what to build as AI agents and open networks
      rewire distribution — then prove the answer on AT Protocol, off-stack,
      before it touches your roadmap.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-8">
      <a href="#contact" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-fg no-underline border-b border-rule pb-0.5 transition-colors duration-150 hover:text-accent hover:border-accent">
        Start a conversation &rarr;
      </a>
      <a href="/thesis/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-fg-tertiary no-underline border-b border-rule pb-0.5 transition-colors duration-150 hover:text-accent hover:border-accent">
        Read the thesis &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE SHIFT -->
  <section class="py-20 fade-in" id="the-shift">
    <hr class="border-t border-rule mb-12" />
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-tertiary mb-12">The shift</p>

    <p class="text-2xl lg:text-4xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight max-w-[640px] mb-16">
      For two decades, product defensibility meant a database behind a
      proprietary interface. The app was how you reached the data. The data was
      the moat.
    </p>

    <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary max-w-[600px] mb-4">
      That model breaks the moment your distribution decentralizes — and it is
      decentralizing from three directions at once.
    </p>

    <div class="max-w-[640px]">
      <div class="border-t border-rule py-8">
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Agents bypass your interface entirely.</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          When an AI agent acts on a user's behalf, it doesn't navigate a UI. It
          needs structured, open data it can parse and transact against. If your
          data is only reachable through your app, you are invisible to the layer
          that is becoming the front door.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-t border-rule py-8">
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">A rented audience is a single point of failure.</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Ask any publisher who built a business on Facebook referral traffic and
          watched it evaporate in one algorithm change. Discovery you don't
          control is a term someone else can revise. When identity and data are
          portable, the audience relationship travels with you instead.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-t border-b border-rule py-8">
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Open networks reward whoever defines the standard.</h3>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          AT Protocol isn't just another feed — it's an open schema layer. The
          companies that define the Lexicons their industry runs on capture the
          ecosystem built on top. Everyone else writes integrations against
          someone else's data model.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- AT Protocol -->
    <p class="text-2xl lg:text-4xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight max-w-[640px] mt-16 mb-12">
      A strategy needs somewhere real to land. Today that is AT Protocol —
      production-grade, not theoretical.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="border-t border-rule pt-8 pb-8 pr-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-accent-blue mb-4">01</p>
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Identity</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Users and brands anchored to domains you already own. Cryptographically
          verifiable, portable across every app on the network, independent of any
          single platform operator.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t md:border-l border-rule pt-8 pb-8 pr-8 md:pl-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-accent-blue mb-4">02</p>
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Distribution</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Publish once, syndicate to any feed, client, or agent on the network.
          Custom algorithms mean you decide how your content is ranked and found —
          not a black box you have no seat at.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t md:border-l border-rule pt-8 pb-8 md:pl-8">
        <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-accent-blue mb-4">03</p>
        <h3 class="text-[17px] font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Schemas</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Lexicons are machine-readable data contracts. Partners, agents, and
          outside developers build against your model directly, with no bespoke
          API surface for you to maintain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Momentum stats -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 border-t border-b border-rule mt-12">
      <div class="py-8 pr-6">
        <div class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-1">43M+</div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg-tertiary leading-snug">Bluesky users</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l border-rule py-8 px-6">
        <div class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-1">$100M</div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg-tertiary leading-snug">Series B, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l border-rule py-8 px-6">
        <div class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-1">IETF</div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg-tertiary leading-snug">Standardization underway</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l border-rule py-8 pl-6">
        <div class="text-3xl font-medium tracking-tight mb-1">1,000+</div>
        <div class="text-[13px] text-fg-tertiary leading-snug">Weekly active apps</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT WE DO -->
  <section class="py-20 fade-in" id="capabilities">
    <hr class="border-t border-rule mb-12" />
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-tertiary mb-12">What we do</p>

    <p class="text-2xl lg:text-4xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight max-w-[640px] mb-16">
      We work with product leaders on the decisions this shift forces: what to
      build, what to open, and what it is worth.
    </p>

    <!-- Strategy -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[180px_1fr] gap-8 md:gap-16 py-10 border-t border-rule items-baseline">
      <div class="text-sm font-medium tracking-tight">Product strategy</div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_200px] gap-8 lg:gap-12">
        <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Where does this shift actually touch your product? We pressure-test
          your distribution surfaces, size what agent-mediated discovery does to
          your funnel, and come back with a position: what to build, what to
          open, what to ignore, and in what order. An argument your exec team
          can decide against, not a deck.
        </p>
        <div class="text-[13px] leading-loose text-fg-tertiary">
          <span class="block font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Deliverables</span>
          <span class="block">Distribution risk assessment</span>
          <span class="block">Opportunity sizing</span>
          <span class="block">Phased roadmap</span>
          <span class="block">Executive narrative</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Architecture -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[180px_1fr] gap-8 md:gap-16 py-10 border-t border-rule items-baseline">
      <div class="text-sm font-medium tracking-tight">Architecture &amp; schemas</div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_200px] gap-8 lg:gap-12">
        <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          A product strategy that can't survive contact with the data model
          isn't one. We design the Lexicons, identity architecture, and feed
          logic underneath the plan — so the thing you decided to build is the
          thing that can actually be built, and agents can read it.
        </p>
        <div class="text-[13px] leading-loose text-fg-tertiary">
          <span class="block font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Deliverables</span>
          <span class="block">Lexicon specifications</span>
          <span class="block">Identity topology</span>
          <span class="block">Federation specs</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Prototyping -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[180px_1fr] gap-8 md:gap-16 py-10 border-t border-b border-rule items-baseline">
      <div class="text-sm font-medium tracking-tight">Off-stack prototyping</div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_200px] gap-8 lg:gap-12">
        <p class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
          Strategy is cheap until someone runs it. We build working feeds,
          integrations, and protocol clients entirely outside your primary
          repos — so you can put real evidence in front of the roadmap
          conversation, and your engineers stay on what they already committed to.
        </p>
        <div class="text-[13px] leading-loose text-fg-tertiary">
          <span class="block font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Deliverables</span>
          <span class="block">Custom algorithmic feeds</span>
          <span class="block">Protocol clients</span>
          <span class="block">Reference implementations</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY A STUDIO -->
  <section class="py-20 fade-in" id="why-studio">
    <hr class="border-t border-rule mb-12" />
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-tertiary mb-12">Why a studio</p>

    <p class="text-2xl lg:text-4xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight max-w-[640px] mb-12">
      The hard part isn't building on a protocol. It's knowing which bet is
      worth making.
    </p>

    <div class="max-w-[600px] space-y-5">
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
        Plenty of teams can ship an integration once someone tells them what to
        ship. The expensive question is the one before that: whether this shift
        genuinely threatens your distribution, which surfaces are exposed, what
        you commit in which order, and what you're willing to open. Those are
        product decisions, and they get made badly when the only people in the
        room are the ones who would build it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
        We bring protocol fluency into that conversation on day one —
        federation, cryptographic identity, schema design — so the strategy is
        grounded in what the technology will actually do, not in what a vendor
        deck claims. Then we build enough of it, off-stack, to find out whether
        the answer holds before you stake a roadmap on it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-fg-secondary">
        And when the work involves defining open standards, neutrality matters. A
        schema authored by one platform gets resisted by its competitors. We sit
        outside that dynamic — which is exactly where this work needs to happen.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CONTACT -->
  <section class="pt-30 pb-20 fade-in" id="contact">
    <hr class="border-t border-rule mb-12" />
    <p class="text-2xl lg:text-4xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight max-w-[560px] mb-6">
      Let's pressure-test your distribution strategy.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-fg-secondary mb-10 leading-relaxed max-w-[460px]">
      Whether you're mapping an 18-month product response to agentic search or
      want an off-stack prototype running on AT Protocol, start the
      conversation. We answer on the network we build on — a direct,
      end-to-end encrypted message.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
      <a
        href="https://bsky.app/profile/birdcandy.com"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="group inline-flex items-center justify-between gap-6 border border-rule px-5 py-4 no-underline transition-colors duration-150 hover:border-fg"
      >
        <span>
          <span class="block text-sm font-medium tracking-tight">Message us on Bluesky</span>
          <span class="block font-mono text-[13px] text-fg-tertiary">@birdcandy.com</span>
        </span>
        <span class="text-fg-tertiary transition-colors duration-150 group-hover:text-fg">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-fg-tertiary max-w-[460px]">
      DMs are end-to-end encrypted via
      <a href="https://www.germnetwork.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="border-b border-rule pb-px transition-colors duration-150 hover:text-accent hover:border-accent">Germ</a>
      — no separate account needed, just your Bluesky handle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- FOOTER -->
  <Footer />

</BaseLayout>
